WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:08.928
Two thousand years ago, an Ancient Greek historian named Callimachus compiled a list of things that he thought were the most amazing and wondrous in the world.

2
00:00:08.932 --> 00:00:12.150
He called it "A Collection of Wonders Around the World."

3
00:00:12.151 --> 00:00:19.997
Not surprisingly, it contained a list of projects undertaken within the Greek Empire, because this was the only world that Callimachus knew.

4
00:00:19.998 --> 00:00:27.655
If Callimachus had traveled the world, perhaps he would have included the Great Wall of China, Stonehenge, or Machu Picchu.

5
00:00:27.656 --> 00:00:33.609
After the fall of the Greek and Roman empires, people forgot about their treasures for hundreds of years.

6
00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:40.990
But as the Middle Ages came to an end, people in Europe rediscovered the literature, art, and philosophy of ancient Greece and Rome.

7
00:00:40.991 --> 00:00:44.757
This revival became known as the Renaissance, which means "rebirth."

8
00:00:44.758 --> 00:00:50.252
During this period, the ancient Greek list of wonders was republished and became famous.

9
00:00:50.253 --> 00:00:56.339
It comprised seven buildings and statues built between 2700 and 270 BC.

10
00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:04.312
Sadly, only one of these has survived, but enough evidence remains for us to take a tour of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.

11
00:01:04.313 --> 00:01:13.095
The first Wonder of the Ancient World is the oldest, and ironically, the only ancient wonder still standing today: the Great Pyramid of Giza in Egypt.

12
00:01:13.096 --> 00:01:19.016
Ancient Egypt was a highly sophisticated civilization ruled by kings who were called pharaohs.

13
00:01:19.017 --> 00:01:31.687
The Great Pyramid was built between approximately 2560 and 2580 BC by the pharaoh Khufu, and, like all pyramids, it was both a monument to, and a tomb for, the pharaoh.

14
00:01:31.688 --> 00:01:39.229
When it was completed, the Great Pyramid was 145 meters high, and for four thousand years it was the world’s tallest building.

15
00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:48.559
It consists of two million stone blocks, each weighing more than two tons, and was covered in a layer of white limestone so that it shone in the desert sun.

16
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:58.082
Today, the mummy and treasure of Pharaoh Khufu are long gone, but the pyramid remains for modern visitors to enjoyㅡ five thousand years

17
00:01:58.092 --> 00:02:05.082
later! The origin of the next Wonder of the Ancient World is a lonely queen’s homesickness for the familiar landscape of her native land.

18
00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:14.068
Nebuchadnezzar II, king of Babylon, which was located in modern-day Iraq, was married to a woman from Medea, now part of northwest Iran.

19
00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:19.352
The king’s wife could not get used to her new home in the flat and barren plains of ancient Iraq.

20
00:02:19.353 --> 00:02:22.566
She longed for the lush, mountainous meadows of ancient Iran.

21
00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:26.382
To please his wife, the king built the Hanging Gardens of Babylon.

22
00:02:26.383 --> 00:02:36.433
Situated on the Euphrates River about fifty kilometers from Baghdad, the gardens took over forty years to build, from 604 to 562 BC.

23
00:02:36.434 --> 00:02:44.646
The Hanging Gardens were a series of terraces where exotic trees, shrubs, and flowers grew, and where even some rare animals lived.

24
00:02:44.647 --> 00:02:51.592
Since it rarely rains in Iraq, the gardens required an elaborate system of irrigation that pumped water from the river.

25
00:02:51.593 --> 00:02:56.676
While the gardens must have been an oasis in the desert, nothing remains of them today.

26
00:02:56.677 --> 00:03:04.085
The next Wonder of the Ancient World has the dual honor of being one of the largest temples in ancient times and of being built twice

27
00:03:04.086 --> 00:03:08.328
. The Temple of Artemis was located near modern-day Izmir in

28
00:03:08.329 --> 00:03:14.076
Turkey. It was constructed to honor the Greek goddess Artemis, the goddess of hunting, nature, and

29
00:03:14.077 --> 00:03:18.020
fertility. The temple was deemed a wonder of the world because of its size and

30
00:03:18.021 --> 00:03:26.753
beauty. It was designed by the Greek architect Chersiphron in 550 BC and was larger than the Parthenon, the famous Greek temple that still stands in

31
00:03:26.754 --> 00:03:38.913
Athens. With its shining white marble, 127 columns, and magnificent art and sculpture, the Temple of Artemis became a popular tourist attraction for local royalty, merchants, and

32
00:03:38.914 --> 00:03:47.787
sightseers. In 356 BC, the temple was destroyed by fire when a man named Herostratus wanted to immortalize himself by ruining this great

33
00:03:47.788 --> 00:03:52.448
edifice. The temple was rebuilt, but was later destroyed by fire once

34
00:03:52.449 --> 00:03:56.451
again. The fourth Wonder of the Ancient World is associated with the Olympic

35
00:03:56.452 --> 00:04:01.562
Games. Did you know that the first Olympic Games were held in ancient Greece more than two thousand years

36
00:04:01.563 --> 00:04:09.465
ago? The ancient Olympic Games were a celebration of both sport and religion, so a temple was included at the site of the games in Olympia,

37
00:04:09.466 --> 00:04:14.552
Greece. As the games grew more popular, a statue to honor the Greek god Zeus was

38
00:04:14.553 --> 00:04:23.375
commissioned. The sculptor Pheidias built a four-story-high statue of Zeus, using wood for the god’s body, and pieces of ivory placed on top for his

39
00:04:23.376 --> 00:04:29.494
skin. The sandals and robe were made of solid gold, and the throne was decorated with precious gems and

40
00:04:29.495 --> 00:04:32.584
ivory. It was said to be the greatest Greek sculpture ever

41
00:04:32.585 --> 00:04:41.907
made. After standing for over eight hundred years at Olympia, it was taken to Constantinople (modern-day Istanbul), where it was later destroyed by

42
00:04:41.908 --> 00:04:49.462
fire. The next Wonder of the Ancient World is the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus, and like the Great Pyramid at Giza, it was a royal

43
00:04:49.463 --> 00:04:55.135
tomb. This enormous white marble tomb was built to hold the remains of Mausolus and his wife,

44
00:04:55.136 --> 00:04:58.501
Artemisia. Mausolus was a provincial king in the Persian

45
00:04:58.502 --> 00:05:04.451
Empire. The tomb was built in 350 BC on a hill that now overlooks the city of Bodrum in modern-day

46
00:05:04.452 --> 00:05:10.792
Turkey. Greek architects designed the forty-meter-high tomb, and four famous Greek sculptors decorated its

47
00:05:10.793 --> 00:05:18.049
walls. On top were massive, carved stone figures of four horses pulling a chariot in which statues of the king and queen

48
00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:27.976
rode. The tomb was so enormous that everyone who saw it was amazed by its grandeur, and the word "mausoleum" became the Greek, and later the English, word for a large

49
00:05:27.977 --> 00:05:33.577
tomb. The next Wonder of the Ancient World was a statue with great political, as well as artistic,

50
00:05:33.578 --> 00:05:40.069
significance. The Colossus of Rhodes was a statue built at the harbor of the city of Rhodes, on the island of Rhodes in ancient

51
00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:47.352
Greece. "Colossus" in Greek means "gigantic statue," and from this word comes the English word "colossal," meaning

52
00:05:47.353 --> 00:05:53.694
"enormous." The huge statue was erected after the city of Rhodes fought off a siege from the nearby country of

53
00:05:53.695 --> 00:06:01.177
Macedonia. The Greek sculptor Chares and the people in his workshop worked twelve years to build a bronze statue in honor of the sun god Helios.

54
00:06:01.178 --> 00:06:07.407
At approximately 36 meters high, it stood almost as high as the Statue of Liberty in the United States.

55
00:06:07.408 --> 00:06:12.592
Helios was posed in a traditional Greek manner: nude and wearing a spiky crown.

56
00:06:12.593 --> 00:06:21.531
Just 56 years after it was built, a strong earthquake shook Rhodes and the colossus broke at its weakest pointㅡthe kneesㅡand collapsed.

57
00:06:21.532 --> 00:06:28.486
The most recent Wonder of the Ancient World is the Lighthouse of Alexandria, the first lighthouse in the world and perhaps the most innovative.

58
00:06:28.487 --> 00:06:32.256
It is the only ancient wonder that also had a practical purpose!

59
00:06:32.257 --> 00:06:37.627
The lighthouse was situated on the island of Pharos in the harbor of the Egyptian city of Alexandria.

60
00:06:37.628 --> 00:06:44.792
Ancient Alexandria was a busy port with dangerous sailing conditions, and so a lighthouse was constructed there in 270 BC.

61
00:06:44.793 --> 00:06:48.630
Modern lighthouses use a massive beam of light powered by electricity.

62
00:06:48.631 --> 00:06:55.666
This ancient lighthouse used curved mirrors to reflect the sun’s rays during the day, and the light of a fire during the night.

63
00:06:55.667 --> 00:06:58.937
Its light could be seen more than fifty kilometers out to sea.

64
00:06:58.938 --> 00:07:06.761
The lighthouse stood for 1,500 years, but after years of earthquakes, it collapsed in 1326 AD.

65
00:07:06.762 --> 00:07:09.499
Where are the Wonders of the Ancient World today?

66
00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:19.995
The unpredictable and powerful forces of nature, as well as the actions of humans, have destroyed all the ancient wonders except for the Great Pyramid of Giza, which still stands today.

67
00:07:19.996 --> 00:07:26.490
However, there is some good news! The British Museum has parts of the Mausoleum of Halicarnassus on display.

68
00:07:26.491 --> 00:07:33.982
The ruins of the Temple of Artemis and the temple which held the statue of Zeus still lie in the grass and rubble where they once stood in Greece.

69
00:07:33.983 --> 00:07:47.634
Archaeologists are investigating whether the Hanging Gardens of Babylon were fact or myth, and while the Colossus of Rhodes was completely destroyed, it was the inspiration for the United States’ most famous attraction, the Statue of Liberty.

70
00:07:47.635 --> 00:07:56.500
Finally, to compensate for the disappearance of the Wonders of the Ancient World, a list of modern wonders was compiled, and you guessed itㅡall of them are standing!

